    "turn_text": "for a long time. you know, I know in America that if I've not been a white guy with the appropriate education, I might not have gotten three chances or two chances or even one chance. So this notion that, you know, we're a country that says, or we value equal opportunity, but for too many people that that doesn't happen. I think it's well about the fact that I was with a group of black ministers a little bit earlier today, an issue that always comes up about the fact that African-American males are pulled over a lot more by the police. I kind of understand that intellectually. But I think about the fact that I've had nine or 10 drivers in the last 10 years, people who work with me as a senator who travel with me and stuff. Two of them met an African-American. They both got pulled over in my neighborhood within the first couple of weeks of working with me. of my white drivers ever got pulled over in my neighborhood for just driving in the neighborhood. So these kind of experiences some of the systemic racism that we see in our country. I think it's important that all people try to speak out about this if they feel they've experienced, because I think sometimes we obviously can't always walk in another shoes, but if we can validate or at least have some understanding of that experience, I think it's important when we're on this moment, when our nation so much needs to heal.",
