    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95507.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, one of the challenges I think we got is for a lot of people of color in our country, and unfortunately for a lot of young people, I think they get frustrated. And they say, well, you know, tap with it. The system is not going to work. Why am I even going to vote? And that just plays into those who advocate for the status quo. And oftentimes, I mean, I see I'm the vice chairman of the Senate Intelligence Committee. So I was the one bipartisan committee that really looked at what's, for example, the Russians did when they tried to intervene in our elections in 2016. You know, a huge amount of the Russian intervention, especially on social media, was geared towards black Americans saying, please don't vote. Well, who do that benefit? Black Americans didn't vote. They just proportionally vote Democratic. That meant that benefited Donald Trump. And, you know, I clearly don't agree with Donald Trump. I think we need a new president. I think about, you know, some of these images that are seared in mind of him trying to use our military to clear away the peaceful protesters. So he could go hold a Bible in front of St. John's Church. And that was especially meaningful for me. That's where my wife Lisa and I got married 31 years ago for him to use Lafayette Square for that photo op was, was I thought just awful. But I hope that people will not be discouraged for better force. The most change you can bring about in our system is when you elect I live in Virginia, as you know. We made major changes in our voting laws, major changes in our civil rights laws, major changes in some of our economic fairness rules, because we changed to control the legislature. When people complain about things in our country at this point, if you don't like some of the things in our country, let's hire a new president. If you don't like things where I work at the United States Senate, well, maybe we ought to have a new majority and not have Senator Mitch McConnell, who's been famous for stopping bills from even getting voted on from continuing to be the guy that gets to choose what we vote on or not. So I do hope there will be a sense that, yeah, let's keep pressure on all of us who are elected right now for change right now. But let's realize, you know, the struggle doesn't end. And if we can't present, provide enough change in the short run, well, give us some more tools and some more tools might be a new president in a different set of folks in charge of the second.",
