  {
    "episode_id": 95507,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95507.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "I'd say to Lauren, that's a great question. You know, there are lots and lots, literally thousands of men and women who work for our intelligence agencies, the CIA and the NSA and part of the FBI and there's 13 different intelligence agencies. And the vast majority of them, they want to do rights right for our country. They're not Democrats, they're not Republicans. Their job is to speak truth to power. And my job as the vice chair of the intelligence committee is to have their back to say, you guys need to tell the truth, no matter what the consequences are. And you're right. I'm worried that this administration and this president, which seems to have so little respect for rule of law has been willing to fire many people that can and they have been willing to speak truth to power. And on the social media companies, I agree as well. I mean, the social media companies, Facebook and Twitter and YouTube have gotten better. I interact with them all the time. They are trying to identify fake foreign folks trying to masquerade as Americans. But why do we have to simply rely on them to do that policing? We ought to set some rules. I think it's outrageous that the CEO of Facebook, Mark Zuckerberg, has basically said, if you're a politician, whether you're Donald Trump or Mark Warner, you can kind of lie with impunity and Facebook will not take anything you say down. I don't think that's First Amendment. I think that is the ability to abuse a platform that Facebook allows you to amplify. You know, it's one thing that Zuckerberg might not want me to stop when I speak in a public square, but Facebook has enormous power to amplify. And so I think there is a need to have some rules of the road. And again, I know I come back to who you hire for these positions. If you want some more rules on the road for social media, you've got to hire some people who are going to be willing to help write those laws.",
