  {
    "episode_id": 95507,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95507.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, that's, again, Kate, that's when I was growing up, you're much younger than me, but when I was growing up, you kind of trusted, there were only a few TV channels, and you kind of trusted the newscasters to tell you the truth. Now let's face it, you can listen to the left or the right, and they have very different versions of the news, and young people right now, if you don't trust the news and then you have these huge number of sites on the internet, most of them with literally no validation, to be an informed consumer today, not just a politics, but of science. You name the topic. It's really hard. I mean, I probably, one of the questions I get the most from young people is, you know, what is the best site that you would trust? You know, I sometimes, I don't have a great answer. I generally say about news, I sometimes trust the BBC, the British Broadcasting Corporation, because they seem like they're a little more neutral. you know, tend to trust NPR, although it's probably got a little more liberal tendencies than some. But, you know, I understand that whether it's MSNBC or Fox, they both come with a very definitive point of view.",
